# gen_fu_mret_prestate_corrigendum.log -- one corrigendum to TWO retained logs (TB Infra)
#
# Corrects gen_fu_l46_irq_step1b.log (md5 e9be074c5036cb62956ff71bc2b29bf7) and
# gen_fu_l47_irq_mret_bin.log (md5 1456cef854e37c0df572b29d05ca9b87). BOTH ARE UNCHANGED; this file stands
# beside them. Raised by rev56 (dv/auto_dv/reviews/ on 03aafce..dc60063), REQUEST-CHANGES.
# One file rather than two, because it is ONE error and the second log inherited it from the first.
#
# ================ WHAT WAS WRONG ================
# The classifier read a retirement's pre-state MIE from the PREVIOUS RETIRED RECORD. That is correct for an
# ordinary instruction and WRONG for a handler's first instruction, because a trap entry clears MIE
# unconditionally (rtl/ibex_cs_registers.sv:924, with mstatus_d.mpie = mstatus_q.mie beside it at :926) and
# RETIRES NO RECORD OF ITS OWN. So for a record with rvfi_intr the previous record is the INTERRUPTED
# instruction, whose MIE was necessarily SET, since that is why the interrupt could be taken.
# gen_nmi_long_directed.S:27-29 builds the storm's vector table as `.rept 31 / mret / .endr`, so every
# regular vector is a BARE mret and every one of the storm's 173 mrets IS a handler's first instruction.
#
# ================ WHAT THE TWO LOGS THEREFORE GET BACKWARDS ================
# gen_fu_l46_irq_step1b.log, "THE MRET EDGE FIX REMOVES A COVERED BIN, AND WHY THAT IS RIGHT": its two-build
# inference concludes that every one of the 173 mrets had MIE set BEFORE it as well as after, so the plan's
# restoring-edge scenario never occurred and the 173 hits were false coverage. The second half of that
# inference is the misread pre-state. The 173 are a TRUE MIE 0 -> 1 restoring edge with MPIE set, MPP M and
# a line pending: exactly the declared bin mret_mpie1_pending. The coverage that log calls false was REAL.
# gen_fu_l47_irq_mret_bin.log, "THE 173, FROM FALSE COVERAGE TO HONEST COVERAGE": the arc is inverted. The
# landing-46 state it calls "false coverage removed" was the correct classification losing its bin, and the
# landing-47 state it calls "honest coverage" was 173 samples booked into a bin whose case never happened.
# The urg figures in both logs are accurate readings of what the code did; it is the INTERPRETATION that is
# wrong, and interpretation is the part a reader cannot re-derive.
#
# ================ WHAT IS TRUE, MEASURED AFTER THE FIX ================
# The fix takes the pre-state from the ENTRY for a record that is a handler's first instruction:
#   bit was = st.is_intr ? 1'b0 : irq_mstatus_prev[ibex_pkg::CSR_MSTATUS_MIE_BIT];
# st.is_trap is deliberately NOT included: that flag marks the instruction that TOOK the fault, whose own
# pre-state really is its predecessor's post-state. Including it would corrupt a case that was correct.
# $ urg, storm run alone, "Total tests in report: 1"
#   cp_mie_global_edge  expected 7  uncovered 5  covered 2
#   uncovered: mret_mie1_mpie1_pending, mret_mpie0_pending, clear_idle, set_idle, clear_pending
#   so the covered two are set_pending and MRET_MPIE1_PENDING, the declared edge bin.
# THE NEW BIN READS ZERO. Its 173 hits were the misclassification.
# $ the four smokes' run summaries, with the discriminating counters this landing adds
#   mrets that are a handler's first instruction: 173 of 173, 6 of 6, 39 of 39, 15 of 15
#   of which wrongly booked into the stays-set bin: 0, 0, 0, 0
# EVERY mret in EVERY one of these programs is a handler's first instruction, which is why the misread
# pre-state was total rather than partial, and why it looked so consistent that a two-build comparison
# seemed to confirm it.
#
# ================ WHY THE TWO-BUILD METHOD DID NOT CATCH IT ================
# Both builds read the same wrong pre-state, so comparing them could only ever confirm what the classifier
# believed. The method looked strong because it used two independent measurements; they were not independent
# of the defect. A comparison across two versions of the SAME classifier cannot test the input both versions
# share. What would have caught it is the discriminator this landing now carries as a counter: asking whether
# the records being classified were entry records at all.
#
# ================ A SECOND CORRECTION TO THE l47 LOG: THE CYCLE-0 RED'S WORDING (Critic L-1) ================
# That log describes MUT-FEDRAIN0 without PRINTING it, and makes THREE cycle-0 claims, not one. Quoted exactly:
#   :120  "Landing 46's valid flag made the drain check judge windows that open at cycle 0, and no red showed it
#          fire for one."                                        -- true, and it is what L-16 asked for
#   :122  "+gen_fetch_en_at_reset=0 so the window IS cycle 0"     -- WRONG, and the worst of the three
#   :128  "The error message names cycle 0 itself, which is what the row asked to see."  -- WRONG
# I originally corrected only the third. The second is the one that misleads most, because it asserts the
# fixture opens a reset window when it does not. The Critic reproduced the red three ways to show both.
# THE MUTATION, printed here as the diff it always should have been (gen_checkers_pkg.sv, the misc monitor's
# fetch_enable arm):
#   -          if (fe_on) begin fe_off_cycle = 0; fe_off_valid = 0; end
#   +          if (fe_on) begin fe_off_cycle = 0; end        // the window never closes
# WHAT THE "cycle 0" IN THE MESSAGE ACTUALLY IS. The mutant leaves the valid flag set while still CLEARING the Off
# cycle, so the drain check runs against a variable holding its cleared value. The 0 in "left On at cycle 0" is that
# value, NOT a window the checker observed at reset.
# AND THE FIXTURE HAS NO RESET WINDOW AT ALL, which is what :122 gets wrong. gen_ut_fetch_en turns the pin On at 5 ns,
# BEFORE reset release, so the plusarg does not give that run a cycle-0 window; the shapes that do open one (the
# reset-reads and lines fixtures) drop it as shorter than GEN_FETCH_EN_WINDOW_MIN_CYCLES, which the window counters
# this landing adds now report as "dropped as shorter than the minimum 1".
# THE RED IS STILL THE RIGHT EVIDENCE, described correctly: it shows the drain rule JUDGING A WINDOW WHOSE CYCLE
# READS ZERO, which is exactly the predicate the valid flag changed, and that is what L-16 asked for. What it does
# not show, and what the l47 log claimed it showed, is a reset-time window being observed. The measured figures are
# unaffected: 38 errors with the rule enabled, 0 with +gen_chk_fetch_en=0, a clean unmutated PASS, and the file
# reverted byte-identical.
#
# ================ WHAT IS NOT CORRECTED ================
# The four-way table itself stands, and rev56 confirms it independently from the RTL (MIE takes MPIE at :956,
# MPIE set at :977, privilege from MPP at :953). The narrowing of the stays-clear arm to require both terms
# is right. Everything else in both logs stands: the render, the mirror and its namespace guard, the derived
# bit, the literal cleanups, the once-per-record view decision, the window counters, the cycle-0 drain red,
# the reset-reads fixture and its four values, and the layout reason for the unhit boot-pc bin.
# END.
